A range loop generates numeric values directly from its loop header.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop 0 to 10 by 2 |value, ordinal|:
        io.line([: [ordinal]: [value]])
    ;
]

### Forms of range loops

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop start to end:
        ...

    loop start to & end |value|:
        ...

    loop start to end by step |value, ordinal|:
        ...

    loop start to & end by step |value, ordinal|:
        ...

    loop to end |value|:
        ...

    loop to & end by step:
        ...
]

### Bounds

- `to` uses an exclusive end bound.
- `to &` uses an inclusive end bound.
- An omitted start is `0`.
- The bounds alone select the counter type.
- There is no `reverse` keyword.
- When start and end are equal, `to` produces no iterations and `to &` produces one.

### Steps

`by` supplies a signed step. Its sign alone selects direction. A positive step
counts upward, a negative step counts downward and a zero step is invalid.
Bounds never infer direction, so a range whose step points away from its end
bound is empty.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop 5 to 0 by -1 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;

    loop 5 to 0 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

The first loop visits `5`, `4`, `3`, `2` and `1`. The second loop visits
nothing.

Range bounds are exact fixed-width integers (`I8` through `U64`) or binary
floats (`F16`, `F32` and `F64`). `Byte`, `Dec` and `DecN` have no range domain.
Mixed bounds follow the exact numeric domain rules in @../numbers (Numbers). The
selected range type is the counter domain, not a positional index type. The step
never selects or widens that counter.

- An omitted step on an integer range is `1` with type `I32`. An untyped whole-number literal step also defaults to `I32`.
- An integer range accepts a step of type `I8`, `I16`, `I32` or `I64`. The step keeps its own type.
- A step whose type is unsigned, binary-float, `Byte` or `Dec` is rejected on an integer range.
- A range using a binary float requires an explicit `by` step.
- A float range accepts a binary-float step at the same or narrower precision than the counter, then converts it to the counter type. A wider float step is rejected. A typed integer step never becomes a float step, while an untyped whole-number literal step takes the counter precision.
- Start and end expressions must have numeric types.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop 0.0 to 1.0 by 0.1 |amount|:
        io.line([: [amount]])
    ;

    loop 1.0 to & 0.0 by -0.5 |amount|:
        io.line([: [amount]])
    ;
]

### Range loop evaluation order

Start, end and explicit step expressions are evaluated once, from left to
right, before the first bounds check.

The loop keeps those evaluated values for the complete iteration.

A dynamic zero step fails before any bounds check, even when the range is empty
or points in the wrong direction.

### Bindings

The first binding receives the current range value in the selected exact counter
type. The bounds alone select that type, and the step keeps its own type. Range
counters keep their selected domain rather than inheriting a minimum width.

The optional second binding receives the zero-based iteration ordinal and always
has type `I32`. It counts visited iterations upward, regardless of the counter
value or direction. The counter domain and ordinal type remain independent.
Collection lengths, collection indices and fixed capacities also use `I32`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop 5 to 0 by -1 |value, ordinal|:
        io.line([: [ordinal]: [value]])
    ;
]

This visits `5` at ordinal `0` through `1` at ordinal `4`.

Bindings use the same one-or-two-name `|...|` rules as collection loops.

### Checked updates

An integer range candidate uses exact arithmetic, and the counter is replaced
only when that candidate still lies within the bound in the step's direction. A
candidate that would pass the bound ends the loop, so the counter never needs a
value outside its domain. An inclusive range exits after visiting its endpoint
without computing a successor.

Termination also holds at the edges of the counter domain. An inclusive range
ending at a type's maximum and a descending range reaching zero both complete
normally. Distances up to `U64::MAX` stay exact, and a step at an `I64` minimum
keeps its full magnitude.

The `I32` ordinal uses a checked integer update, so its own overflow remains an
implicit failure.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    near_max U8 = 254
    maximum U8 = 255
    loop near_max to & maximum by 1 |value, ordinal|:
        io.line([: [ordinal]: [value]])
    ;
]

This range visits `254` and `255` as `U8` values. Its `I32` ordinal runs from
`0` to `1`, and termination needs no `U8` successor beyond `255`.

A literal zero step is rejected during compilation with `MOTH-SYNTAX-0029`, and
a dynamic zero step uses built-in error code 306 (`InvalidLoopStep`).

Floating-point ranges round each candidate to the selected binary precision before
checking the endpoint. A non-finite or out-of-range candidate ends the loop
without becoming the next value. If an in-range candidate rounds back to the
current value while the endpoint still lies ahead, the loop fails at runtime
with built-in error code 307 (`RangeStepNoProgress`) rather than repeating that
value.

Const range loops diagnose statically known zero and no-progress steps while
folding. @../constants/#const-templates (Const templates) owns those compile-time
rules.

@../progress (Progress) records target support separately from these range
semantics.
